{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","statement":"Adult acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a type of cancer in which the bone marrow makes too many lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell.","source_span":"Adult acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a type of cancer in which the bone marrow makes too many lymphocytes (a type of white blood cell)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","statement":"Adult ALL is also called acute lymphocytic leukemia.","source_span":"also called acute lymphocytic leukemia"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","statement":"Adult ALL usually gets worse quickly if it is not treated.","source_span":"This type of cancer usually gets worse quickly if it is not treated."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","statement":"Previous chemotherapy and exposure to radiation may increase the risk of developing adult ALL.","source_span":"Previous chemotherapy and exposure to radiation may increase the risk of developing ALL."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","statement":"Signs and symptoms of adult ALL include fever, feeling tired, and easy bruising or bleeding.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms of adult ALL include fever, feeling tired, and easy bruising or bleeding."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","statement":"Adult ALL can spread to the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord).","source_span":"The cancer can also spread to the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","statement":"In Philadelphia chromosome positive ALL, part of one chromosome switches places with part of another chromosome, a change called the Philadelphia chromosome.","source_span":"in Philadelphia chromosome positive ALL, part of one chromosome switches places with part of another chromosome. This is called the Philadelphia chromosome."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","statement":"Prognosis and treatment options for adult ALL depend on the patient's age, whether the cancer has spread to the brain or spinal cord, whether there are certain gene changes including the Philadelphia chromosome, and whether the cancer has been treated before or has recurred.","source_span":"The prognosis (chance of recovery) and treatment options depend on the following: - The age of the patient. - Whether the cancer has spread to the brain or spinal cord. - Whether there are certain changes in the genes, including the Philadelphia chromosome. - Whether the cancer has been treated before or has recurr"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","statement":"Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy for diagnosing adult ALL involves removal of bone marrow, blood, and a small piece of bone by inserting a hollow needle into the hipbone or breastbone.","source_span":"The removal of bone marrow, blood, and a small piece of bone by inserting a hollow needle into the hipbone or breastbone."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","statement":"Other symptoms of adult ALL include petechiae, shortness of breath, weight loss or loss of appetite, bone or stomach pain, painless lumps in the neck/underarm/stomach/groin, and having many infections.","source_span":"Petechiae (flat, pinpoint spots under the skin, caused by bleeding). - Shortness of breath. - Weight loss or loss of appetite. ... Painless lumps in the neck, underarm, stomach, or groin. - Having many infections."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What is adult acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)?","answer":"Adult ALL, also called acute lymphocytic leukemia, is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow in which the bone marrow makes too many lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What two things may increase the risk of developing adult ALL?","answer":"Previous chemotherapy and exposure to radiation may increase the risk of developing adult ALL."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","fact_refs":[4,10],"question":"What are common signs and symptoms of adult ALL?","answer":"Common signs and symptoms include fever, feeling tired, easy bruising or bleeding, petechiae, shortness of breath, weight loss or loss of appetite, bone or stomach pain, painless lumps in the neck, underarm, stomach, or groin, and having many infections."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What is the Philadelphia chromosome in the context of adult ALL?","answer":"In Philadelphia chromosome positive ALL, part of one chromosome switches places with part of another chromosome; this genetic change is called the Philadelphia chromosome."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What four factors affect the prognosis and treatment options for adult ALL?","answer":"Prognosis and treatment depend on the patient's age, whether the cancer has spread to the brain or spinal cord, whether there are certain gene changes including the Philadelphia chromosome, and whether the cancer has been treated before or has recurred."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_1","fact_refs":[8],"question":"How is a bone marrow aspiration and biopsy performed to help diagnose adult ALL?","answer":"It involves removing bone marrow, blood, and a small piece of bone by inserting a hollow needle into the hipbone or breastbone, then examining the samples under a microscope."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_2","statement":"Adult acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer in which the bone marrow makes abnormal myeloblasts (a type of white blood cell), red blood cells, or platelets.","source_span":"Adult acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer in which the bone marrow makes abnormal myeloblasts (a type of white blood cell), red blood cells, or platelets."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_2","statement":"AML is the most common type of acute leukemia in adults.","source_span":"It is the most common type of acute leukemia in adults."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_2","statement":"AML is also called acute myelogenous leukemia, acute myeloblastic leukemia, acute granulocytic leukemia, and acute nonlymphocytic leukemia.","source_span":"AML is also called acute myelogenous leukemia, acute myeloblastic leukemia, acute granulocytic leukemia, and acute nonlymphocytic leukemia."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_2","statement":"Possible risk factors for AML include being male, smoking especially after age 60, having had treatment with chemotherapy or radiation therapy in the past, having had treatment for childhood ALL, being exposed to radiation from an atomic bomb or the chemical benzene, and having a history of a blood disorder such as myelodysplastic syndrome.","source_span":"Possible risk factors for AML include the following: - Being male. - Smoking, especially after age 60. - Having had treatment with chemotherapy or radiation therapy in the past. - Having had treatment for childhood acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) in the past. - Being exposed to radiation from an atomic bomb or to the chemical benzene. - Having a history of a blood disorder such as myelodysplastic syndrome."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_2","statement":"Acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) is a subtype of AML that occurs when parts of two genes stick together, usually in middle-aged adults, with signs that may include both bleeding and forming blood clots.","source_span":"Acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) is a subtype of AML that occurs when parts of two genes stick together. APL usually occurs in middle-aged adults. Signs of APL may include both bleeding and forming blood clots."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_2","statement":"Signs and symptoms of adult AML include fever, shortness of breath, easy bruising or bleeding, petechiae, weakness or feeling tired, and weight loss or loss of appetite.","source_span":"Fever. - Shortness of breath. - Easy bruising or bleeding. - Petechiae (flat, pinpoint spots under the skin caused by bleeding). - Weakness or feeling tired. - Weight loss or loss of appetite."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_2","statement":"Fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) may be done along with cytogenetic analysis to look for certain changes in the chromosomes when diagnosing AML.","source_span":"Other tests, such as fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH), may also be done to look for certain changes in the chromosomes."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_2","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What is adult acute myeloid leukemia (AML)?","answer":"Adult AML is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow in which the bone marrow makes abnormal myeloblasts, red blood cells, or platelets; it is the most common type of acute leukemia in adults."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_2","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are other names for adult acute myeloid leukemia?","answer":"AML is also called acute myelogenous leukemia, acute myeloblastic leukemia, acute granulocytic leukemia, and acute nonlymphocytic leukemia."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_2","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What are the possible risk factors for adult AML?","answer":"Possible risk factors include being male, smoking (especially after age 60), past chemotherapy or radiation therapy, past treatment for childhood ALL, exposure to atomic bomb radiation or the chemical benzene, and a history of a blood disorder such as myelodysplastic syndrome."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_2","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What is acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) and what are its signs?","answer":"APL is a subtype of AML that occurs when parts of two genes stick together, usually in middle-aged adults; its signs may include both bleeding and forming blood clots."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_2","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What are the symptoms of adult AML?","answer":"Symptoms include fever, shortness of breath, easy bruising or bleeding, petechiae, weakness or feeling tired, and weight loss or loss of appetite."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_2","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What laboratory technique may be used alongside cytogenetic analysis to detect chromosome changes in AML?","answer":"Fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) may be done along with cytogenetic analysis to look for certain changes in the chromosomes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_3","statement":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) is a type of cancer in which the bone marrow makes too many lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell.","source_span":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia is a type of cancer in which the bone marrow makes too many lymphocytes (a type of white blood cell)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_3","statement":"CLL is a blood and bone marrow disease that usually gets worse slowly, is one of the most common types of leukemia in adults, and rarely occurs in children.","source_span":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (also called CLL) is a blood and bone marrow disease that usually gets worse slowly. CLL is one of the most common types of leukemia in adults. It often occurs during or after middle age; it rarely occurs in children."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_3","statement":"Risk factors for CLL include being middle-aged or older, male, or white; a family history of CLL or cancer of the lymph system; and having relatives who are Russian Jews or Eastern European Jews.","source_span":"Risk factors for CLL include the following: - Being middle-aged or older, male, or white. - A family history of CLL or cancer of the lymph system. - Having relatives who are Russian Jews or Eastern European Jews."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_3","statement":"CLL usually does not cause any signs or symptoms and is often found during a routine blood test; its main signs and symptoms are swollen lymph nodes and tiredness.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms of chronic lymphocytic leukemia include swollen lymph nodes and tiredness. Usually CLL does not cause any signs or symptoms and is found during a routine blood test."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_3","statement":"Additional symptoms of CLL include painless swelling of the lymph nodes in the neck, underarm, stomach, or groin, feeling very tired, pain or fullness below the ribs, fever and infection, and weight loss for no known reason.","source_span":"Painless swelling of the lymph nodes in the neck, underarm, stomach, or groin. - Feeling very tired. - Pain or fullness below the ribs. - Fever and infection. - Weight loss for no known reason."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_3","statement":"Patients with an IgVH gene mutation have a better prognosis.","source_span":"Patients with an IgVH gene mutation have a better prognosis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_3","statement":"Diagnostic tests for CLL include immunophenotyping, FISH (fluorescence in situ hybridization), flow cytometry, the IgVH gene mutation test, and bone marrow aspiration and biopsy.","source_span":"Immunophenotyping ... FISH (fluorescence in situ hybridization) ... Flow cytometry ... IgVH gene mutation test ... Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_3","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What is chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)?","answer":"CLL is a blood and bone marrow cancer in which the bone marrow makes too many lymphocytes; it usually gets worse slowly, is one of the most common leukemias in adults, and rarely occurs in children."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_3","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are the risk factors for developing CLL?","answer":"Risk factors include being middle-aged or older, male, or white; having a family history of CLL or cancer of the lymph system; and having relatives who are Russian Jews or Eastern European Jews."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_3","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How is CLL often first discovered, given that it usually causes no symptoms?","answer":"CLL usually does not cause any signs or symptoms and is often found during a routine blood test."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_3","fact_refs":[3,4],"question":"What are common signs and symptoms of CLL?","answer":"Common signs and symptoms include swollen lymph nodes and tiredness, plus painless swelling of lymph nodes in the neck, underarm, stomach, or groin, pain or fullness below the ribs, fever and infection, and unexplained weight loss."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_3","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What is the significance of an IgVH gene mutation for CLL patients?","answer":"Patients with an IgVH gene mutation have a better prognosis."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_3","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What laboratory tests are used to diagnose CLL?","answer":"Diagnostic tests include immunophenotyping, FISH, flow cytometry, the IgVH gene mutation test, and bone marrow aspiration and biopsy."}
